{% extends 'layouts/base.twig' %}

{% block content %}
  {#
    The index is a table that behaves like a poster: number flush left, title at
    display size, year and place flush right. No thumbnails — the point of the page
    is the shape of the body of work, not a preview of it.
  #}
  <section class="shell masthead">
    <p class="label label--ink">Index</p>
    <h1 class="title" style="margin-top: 0.75rem">Projects</h1>

    <div class="masthead__meta">
      {# An id, so the count is swapped with the results rather than left stale. #}
      <p class="label" id="project-count">{{ posts|length }} series</p>
      {% if archive_description %}
        <div class="label">{{ archive_description|striptags|trim }}</div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </section>

  {#
    Filtering, with nothing WordPress does not already parse.

    `project_category[]` is the taxonomy's own query var. WP_Query reads it whether it
    arrives as an array from a checkbox group or as `?project_category=a,b`, sorts the
    terms and builds one IN clause — so this form needs no hook, no manager and no
    custom parameter. `orderby` is a public query var for the same reason.

    Without JavaScript this submits and the page reloads filtered. `Fetch` enhances
    that: it fetches the same URL and swaps the elements whose `id` it finds in the
    response, which here are the index and the count.

    Deliberately no `data-option-src`, unlike the pagination links below, which point
    at a section URL. Both are cached — `foehn_sections` is a cache-key query argument —
    so the choice is about how many files a filter combination occupies rather than
    about whether it is cached at all. Fetching the whole page and keeping one div of it
    reuses the file the no-JS path already warmed.

    `selector` is named rather than left at its default, and this is not a
    micro-optimisation. The default is `[id]`, and the layout wraps this form in
    `#main` and `#demo-app` — so every fetch replaced the form with the server's copy
    of it and threw away whatever was ticked. Two quick clicks then raced, and the
    first response to arrive rewrote the second checkbox back to unchecked. Naming the
    two regions that change keeps the controls out of the swap, which is also what
    lets somebody keep clicking while a request is still in the air.

    `fetch()` is called with no URL, because the component builds one itself: since
    1.11.0 it folds *every* value of a repeated field onto the action, which is what a
    checkbox group is made of. Ticking a second series adds a second
    `project_category[]` to the request, exactly as the no-JS submit does.

    `abort()` before each request makes the last click the one that wins. Two requests
    in flight together are otherwise a race the network settles, and the earlier answer
    can arrive last and rewrite the listing under the boxes that are ticked.
  #}
  <form
    method="get"
    action="{{ fn('get_post_type_archive_link', 'project') }}"
    class="shell filters"
    data-component="Fetch Action"
    data-option-history
    data-option-selector="#foehn-section-project-index, #project-count"
    data-on:change="() => { Fetch.abort(); Fetch.fetch() }">
    {#
      `facet()` is what makes these options a facet rather than a list: each one knows
      how many results it would give in the view being looked at, and an option that
      would give none says so instead of leaving the visitor to find out.

      The counts come from the current filters with *this* taxonomy's own filter lifted.
      Left in, picking one series would report zero for every other series and a visitor
      could never pick a second one — see Studiometa\Foehn\Query\Facets.
    #}
    <fieldset class="filters__group">
      <legend class="label label--ink">Series</legend>

      {#
        Checkboxes, and several may be ticked at once: `project_category[]` reaches
        WP_Query as an array and becomes one `IN` clause, so two series show the union
        of both. Radios would be the honest control for a single choice, and this is
        not one — narrowing to one series is what clicking a series on a project page
        already does.
      #}

      {% for option in facet('project_category') %}
        <label class="filters__option {{ option.isEmpty ? 'filters__option--empty' }}">
          <input
            type="checkbox"
            name="project_category[]"
            value="{{ option.term.slug }}"
            {{ option.active ? 'checked' }}
            {# A dead end is disabled rather than hidden: the set of filters should not
               move around as the visitor uses it. An already-checked one stays usable,
               or there would be no way to undo it. #}
            {{ option.isEmpty and not option.active ? 'disabled' }} />
          {{ option.term.name }}
          {% if option.count is not null %}
            <span class="filters__count">{{ option.count }}</span>
          {% endif %}
        </label>
      {% endfor %}
    </fieldset>

    {#
      `orderby` and `order` are both public query vars, so this pair needs no more
      configuration than the series do. The default is neither of them: with nothing
      asked for, `ThemeHooks` orders the archive by the curated sequence.
    #}
    <label class="filters__group">
      <span class="label label--ink">Sort by</span>
      <select name="orderby">
        <option value="date" {{ query_get('orderby', 'date') == 'date' ? 'selected' }}>Date</option>
        <option value="title" {{ query_get('orderby') == 'title' ? 'selected' }}>Title</option>
      </select>
    </label>

    {# Lowercase, because the page cache keys the value and `DESC` would be a second
       spelling of the same page. WordPress reads either. #}
    <label class="filters__group">
      <span class="label label--ink">Order</span>
      <select name="order">
        <option value="desc" {{ query_get('order', 'desc')|lower == 'desc' ? 'selected' }}>Descending</option>
        <option value="asc" {{ query_get('order')|lower == 'asc' ? 'selected' }}>Ascending</option>
      </select>
    </label>

    {#
      The one filter here that WordPress will not read from a URL by itself.
      `posts_per_page` is a private query var — a visitor who can set it can ask for
      every post on the site — so `QueryFiltersHook` makes it public against the
      allowlist in `app/query-filters.config.php`, and a value outside that list is
      refused rather than clamped.
    #}
    <label class="filters__group">
      <span class="label label--ink">Per page</span>
      <select name="posts_per_page">
        {% for size in [3, 6, 12] %}
          <option value="{{ size }}" {{ query_get('posts_per_page', 3) == size ? 'selected' }}>{{ size }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
    </label>

    {# The no-JS path, and the way out of a filter. Both are plain HTML. #}
    <div class="filters__actions">
      <button type="submit">Filter</button>
      {% if query_all() is not empty %}
        <a href="{{ query_url_clear() }}" data-component="Fetch" data-option-history>Reset</a>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </form>

  {{ foehn_section('project-index', { posts, pagination }) }}
{% endblock %}
